o compare. Perhaps we can argue that Candidate D's strong concentration and problem solving, plus responsibility, offset his arrogance, making him a viable alternative, and his attention may be better than B's weak memory. So we could shift vote to D.\n\nBut we need to be careful: we must use explicit facts. For D: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical competence), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concerns: regarded as arrogant (crew cooperation), relatively weak leadership skills (crew cooperation). No mention of memory issues. So D may have better attention.\n\nThus we could argue D is a stronger overall fit than B because attention and responsibility are critical, and his stress resilience is adequate. While his arrogance is a concern, it may be mitigated.\n\nThus we could change recommendation to D.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: Agent 3 recommended B, Agent 2 agreed but raised concerns. We'll say we appreciate those concerns and propose D as a better balance.\n\nLet's craft public message: \"I understand the strengths of B, but his weak memory for numbers and nasty remarks raise serious concerns about attention and crew cooperation. Candidate D, on the other hand, demonstrates strong concentration, problem solving, and responsibility, with adequate response to unexpected events, and while he is seen as arrogant and has weak leadership, these may be more manageable than B's attention deficits. Therefore I would shift my vote to Candidate D.\"\n\nWe need to include explicit facts: D can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, responds to unexpected events adequately. B has weak memory for numbers, nasty remarks, can be grumpy/uncooperative. Provide implication.\n\nNow produce metadata JSON with vote \"Candidate D\". Ensure format: {\"agent\": \"agent_1\", \"vote\": \"Candidate D\"}.\n\nP